course: dictionary
lesson: 12167
title: Words with more than one meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. To find the right one, read the words around it. Those words are the context, and they leave clues. «Comprehensive» is one of these words.
explain: «Mia wrote a comprehensive guide to birds.» The clue is guide to birds. A guide that covers everything about birds is complete and thorough, so comprehensive means complete. Ask yourself: does the sentence need everything, or just a little?
explain: The same word can mean something different in other places. In the UK, «Sam goes to a comprehensive» means he goes to a state secondary school that takes children of all abilities. In sport, «The home team had a comprehensive win» means a very big, clear win.
fact: Comprehensive means including everything or almost everything that is needed; complete and thorough.
fact: In the UK, a comprehensive is a state secondary school that takes children of all abilities without an entrance test.
fact: In sport, a comprehensive win is a very big, clear win.
fact: Comprehensive car insurance covers damage to your own car as well as to other people's.
fact: A good check is to swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
exercise: «Mia wrote a comprehensive guide to birds.» What does comprehensive mean here? | Noisy and loud | *Complete, with everything needed | Very small and short | Hidden under the bed
exercise: In the UK, what is a comprehensive? | A very small primary school | A test children take to enter a grammar school | A shop that sells everything | *A state secondary school that takes children of all abilities
exercise: «The home team had a comprehensive win.» What does comprehensive win suggest? | A win that was cancelled | A short, close game | *A very big, clear win | A draw with no goals
test: «Our teacher gave us comprehensive revision notes.» What does comprehensive mean? | Written in pencil | Too short to read | *Complete and thorough | Lost somewhere in the classroom
test: Which school takes children of all abilities without an entrance test? | *Comprehensive school | Village nursery | Private boarding school | Grammar school
exam: «Comprehensive car insurance covers damage to your own car as well as to other people's.» What does comprehensive mean here? | Cheap and small | Only for bicycles | Written by a teacher | *Covering everything that is needed, complete
